<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Mind View — the nested labwc a mind's apps are drawn in (#239).

  Run by yantrik-ui as `labwc -C /opt/yantrik/share/labwc-mind -s <seat script>` with
  WLR_BACKENDS=wayland: on the person's compositor it is one ordinary window, and inside that
  window is a display of its own. An app a mind opens is started on that display, so it lands in
  Mind View instead of over the person's work. See crates/yantrik-ui/src/mind_view.rs.

  The same theme as the person's desktop (config/labwc/rc.xml), so a window reads as this OS's
  wherever it is drawn. What differs is deliberate:

  - A <margin> at the top, and only there: the Mind desk's bar (#1023, apps/mind-desk) lives in it.
    Nothing else in here reserves space, so there is no status bar or taskbar.
  - Two keybindings, not the desktop's thirty. The person's compositor sees every key first, so
    their own shortcuts — lock, screenshot, the launcher — keep working while Mind View has the
    keyboard; the only keys that reach this one are ones the person's compositor did not bind.
    Defining any <keybind> also stops labwc loading its stock set, whose Super+Return starts a
    terminal nobody installed.
-->
<labwc_config>
  <theme>
    <name>Yantrik</name>
    <cornerRadius>10</cornerRadius>
    <dropShadows>no</dropShadows>
    <font place="ActiveWindow">
      <name>Barlow</name>
      <size>10</size>
      <weight>medium</weight>
    </font>
    <font place="InactiveWindow">
      <name>Barlow</name>
      <size>10</size>
    </font>
    <font place="MenuItem">
      <name>Barlow</name>
      <size>10</size>
    </font>
  </theme>

  <!-- As on the desktop: an agent moving the pointer must not move the keyboard. -->
  <focus>
    <followMouse>no</followMouse>
  </focus>

  <!--
    The Mind desk's bar (#1023): the header, the tool strip and the footer along the top, 160px
    (76 + 40 + 44). A maximised window fills what is below it. See the window rule.
  -->
  <margin top="160" bottom="0" left="0" right="0" />

  <!--
    Every window fills Mind View. It is a view onto what the mind is doing, not a second desktop
    to arrange: Mind View opens at 70% of the person's screen (mind_view::SIZE_PERCENT, about
    896×560 on the VM's 1280×800), and an app opening at its preferred 1100×800 in there was
    mostly off its edge, so the person watching saw a title bar and a corner. Our apps ask for at
    least 640×400, which a maximized window in Mind View always has room for.
  -->
  <windowRules>
    <windowRule identifier="*">
      <action name="Maximize" />
    </windowRule>

    <!--
      The Mind desk (#1023, apps/mind-desk): one window, a bar along the top of Mind View, over
      the first pixel of the apps' area and as wide as the display at every size. A window the
      height of Mind View would sit over the apps and take their clicks; a bar leaves them the rest.

      The numbers are apps/mind-desk's `metrics` (its tests read this file and fail if they drift):
      the margin is the bar's height less one pixel, and the window is 161px.

      Why these actions, in this order, found by running labwc 0.8.3 (tests/mind-desk-placement
      does it again and fails if a bar is not where it belongs, at 896x560 and at 1280x690 and
      after a restart):
      - MoveTo places a window at an absolute position at its first map; MoveToEdge does nothing
        there, and MoveRelative then moves it from where it was centred. Nothing here is a screen
        size: the bar is at the top, which is 0 at every size.
      - ToggleMaximize horizontally is what makes the bar as wide as the display, now and after
        the display is resized. labwc keeps such a window at its stored position, and re-centres
        it if that position does not touch the usable area, which the margin makes it not: so the
        bar is one pixel taller than the margin, and the pixel keeps it where it is. A second
        MoveTo puts the window itself back, as the toggle moves it.
      - fixedPosition: nothing moves it afterwards, a Mind dragging it with a modifier included.
      - Always on top, no title bar, not in Alt+Tab or the taskbar: it is the room's wall, not a
        window in it. Matched by app id and title, which the desk sets before it maps. Any client
        can set its own, so a window in here that sets both can be placed like the bar and drawn
        over the real one: labwc cannot tell them apart; setting both is what it takes. Nothing the desk is trusted for
        rests on this. The shell answers the one desk process it started (pid and start time),
        and a stop is asked of the person on their own desktop, never in here. A forged bar can
        only mislead the eye.
      - Never force-maximised: the rule above matches every window and has already run, so the
        first thing this rule does is undo it (rules run in order, and every one that matches
        runs).
    -->
    <windowRule identifier="yantrik-mind-desk" title="Yantrik Mind Desk" serverDecoration="no" skipTaskbar="yes" skipWindowSwitcher="yes" fixedPosition="yes">
      <action name="UnMaximize" />
      <action name="MoveTo" x="0" y="0" />
      <action name="ToggleMaximize" direction="horizontal" />
      <action name="MoveTo" x="0" y="0" />
      <action name="ToggleAlwaysOnTop" />
    </windowRule>
  </windowRules>

  <keyboard>
    <keybind key="A-Tab">
      <action name="NextWindow" />
    </keybind>
    <keybind key="A-F4">
      <action name="Close" />
    </keybind>
  </keyboard>
</labwc_config>
